00:26:02,240 --> 00:26:12,300
 So to me, this helps to understand how durable functions actually work when you look at it just like a step functions workflow, because that's basically what it is.

213
00:26:13,820 --> 00:26:17,020
 And this is every path that the code can take in this case.

214
00:26:17,340 --> 00:26:19,840
 It shows us what the code is doing.

215
00:26:20,300 --> 00:26:27,920
 The execution history, on the other hand, it shows the path that a real conversation, a real execution actually took.

216
00:26:27,920 --> 00:26:33,180
 And that's something that you can see in the AWS console when you go into a durable function.

217
00:26:33,960 --> 00:26:35,660
 So I'm going to show you what that looks like.

218
00:26:36,440 --> 00:26:41,000
 So let's look at execution history through a conversation.

219
00:26:42,400 --> 00:26:49,520
 So the first message starts the execution in our durable execution.

220
00:26:50,100 --> 00:26:53,620
 So route 1 is the routing model's decision, checkpointed.

221
00:26:53,620 --> 00:27:05,860
 Handle one, we are now calling one of the specialists, it is invoked as a separate Lambda function, and then send reply, the reply, the message that gets sent once.

222
00:27:06,580 --> 00:27:12,280
 And then the customer answers, Thursday morning, that's going to be reply two, now we're in the loop, in that loop.

223
00:27:12,820 --> 00:27:20,180
 The callback, the webhook completed with their message, and then handle two, the second specialist term, and the confirmation.

