00:27:29,040 --> 00:27:33,800
 And that's one path through the map on the previous slide where we saw the workflow.

227
00:27:35,080 --> 00:27:42,640
 So let's just look at some of the things that I learned out of building these kind of workflows.

228
00:27:44,500 --> 00:27:51,020
 I've had this specific agent running and we've had customers actually using it

229
00:27:51,020 --> 00:27:55,260
 and sending messages back and forth.

230
00:27:55,400 --> 00:27:58,740
 And the durable side of things, it never failed.

231
00:27:58,740 --> 00:28:03,140
 The checkpoints, the callbacks, all the resumes just worked.

232
00:28:03,780 --> 00:28:06,840
 What did fail many times was the model.

233
00:28:07,740 --> 00:28:10,720
 Sometimes the reply was confidently wrong,

234
00:28:11,240 --> 00:28:14,300
 a time that didn't exist, a booking it never made.

235
00:28:15,660 --> 00:28:20,120
 Infrastructure reports success, but the customer gets the wrong answer.

236
00:28:20,920 --> 00:28:25,080
 Durable execution isn't gonna fix that part of things.

237
00:28:25,080 --> 00:28:27,980
 So we need to do a few things to make sure

238
00:28:27,980 --> 00:28:30,280
 that it actually replies correctly.

239
00:28:30,280 --> 00:28:34,680
 It is non-deterministic as we heard earlier today as well.

240
00:28:34,680 --> 00:28:37,600
 So what we can do is add a gate before the send.

241
00:28:37,600 --> 00:28:40,600
 So the specialist returns the structured result

242
00:28:40,600 --> 00:28:42,840
 and you check it against the booking system

243
00:28:42,840 --> 00:28:45,480
 before you actually send the message.

244
00:28:45,480 --> 00:28:47,180
 Then you send it.

245
00:28:47,180 --> 00:28:49,860
 And recoverable can still be wrong.

246
00:28:49,860 --> 00:28:53,800
 The workflow comes back, but the answer doesn't get any better.

247
00:28:54,640 --> 00:28:57,940
 And second, choose a model per specialist.

248
00:28:58,260 --> 00:29:02,860
 So when we break it down into multiple agents like this, with multiple specialists,

249
00:29:03,420 --> 00:29:06,960
 we can then set models based on what they actually need to do.

250
00:29:07,120 --> 00:29:09,520
 Some models can be fast, cheap models.

251
00:29:10,140 --> 00:29:14,800
 Some can be more reasoning-type models that give you a better answer in return.

252
00:29:17,020 --> 00:29:21,020
 And the execution history, it told me that the workflow

253
00:29:21,020 --> 00:29:25,020
 succeeded, the one we saw before, but it doesn't tell me if the answer

254
00:29:25,020 --> 00:29:29,020
 that it actually gave was true.

255
00:29:29,020 --> 00:29:33,020
 So, I want to show you, this is kind of a

